// The simulated world a stack's robots run in, as one MCP surface: the
// scene (assets, the objects standing in it and their poses, the loaded
// scene, the robots and their placement), the controls its objects offer
// (a desk's height), its light sources, the optical properties of its
// materials, a picture of it from any viewpoint, its clock (the time now, a
// pause, a step), and where a robot can work in it (the surfaces and objects
// its arms reach and its perception camera sees). Nothing here has a
// counterpart on the physical robot; the title says so, the instructions
// open by saying so, and every description says so, so a model reading this
// endpoint never mistakes it for a real-robot surface. Waldo alone
// implements object_controls, scene_lighting, scene_materials, scene_view,
// simulation_clock and scene_workspace, so only a Waldo stack serves this
// document; the robots' own surface is the robot_control document, served
// on hardware and in simulation alike. The inventory
// scene.get_objects_list reports, and where workspace.* says a robot can
// work, are the world's setup, for a caller that checks it; a robot's task
// still finds its items through the robot's own perception. The velocities
// of the same objects, streamed for evaluation, are the engine's
// object_state contract, which stays inside the peppy framework, as the
// README's ground-truth rule says.
//
// This document is the whole artifact. A launcher lists it under
// `source: { exposures: ["simulation:v1"] }`, binds its seven targets to
// the running simulation through `links`, and the server built into peppy
// serves it at `/simulation/v1/mcp`. The launchers hub serves it through
// the `world_control` axis of its `simulation_mcp` launcher, on port 8902, a
// process of its own beside the robots' endpoint `/robot_control/v1/mcp`
// on port 8900. It binds the simulation and nothing of any robot copy, so
// it outlives the copy.
{
  peppy_schema: "mcp_exposure/v1",
  manifest: { name: "simulation", tag: "v1" },

  // Advertised to clients through `server/discover`. `instructions` is the
  // prose a model reads before it decides what to call. It is where the
  // model learns the id scheme, the units, the scale bound, the
  // read-then-set pattern and the rules several tools share. A tool's own
  // bounds are in its description. The `restrict` bounds of a service are
  // in its input schema too. None of the contracts' comments reach the
  // model. The opening sentence and the title suffix are the wording rule
  // that test_no_simulation_ground_truth.py enforces on every document under
  // simulation/.
  server: {
    title: "Simulated world (simulation only)",
    instructions: "This endpoint configures a simulated world. It has no effect on and no counterpart in the physical robot. The tools edit the simulation the robots run in: scene.* for the scene (what the engine can place, the loaded scene, the objects standing in it and their poses, the robots standing and their placement), controls.* for what an object lets you set (an adjustable desk's height, a conveyor's belt speed), lighting.* for its light sources, materials.* for the optical properties of its materials, scene.look for a picture of the world from any viewpoint, scene.preview_asset for the catalogue's picture of an asset before you place it, clock.* for the simulation's clock, and workspace.* for where a robot can work in it. Use it to set the world up and to check the setup, never to complete a task: to move an object, grasp it with a robot through the robots' endpoint; scene.move_object teleports and proves nothing about the robot, and scene.get_objects_list checks a setup and completes no task. Read, then set: call scene.get_asset_categories then scene.get_assets_list, scene.get_robots_list, controls.get_object_controls, lighting.get_lighting and materials.get_materials first, since every asset_id, robot, control id, light_id and material_id the other tools take comes from them, and every object_id is minted by scene.spawn_object or listed by scene.get_objects_list. Nothing falls, drops or settles while the simulation is paused. To arrange things without the physics moving them, call clock.set_paused with paused true, and place or remove them. Then call clock.step before you read positions with scene.get_objects_list or scene.get_robots_list, or call workspace.describe or workspace.check. After you load or arrange a world, and before a robot starts its actions, call workspace.describe to learn where on each work surface a robot can work and workspace.check to confirm that it can work the objects a task needs where they stand. Positions are world-frame [x, y, z] in metres, Z up; headings are a yaw in radians about the world's +Z, counterclockwise seen from above, 0 facing +X; forces are world-frame newtons; masses are kilograms; durations are seconds; scale is uniform and 1 means as authored; directions are world-frame unit vectors pointing the way the light travels; colours are linear RGB in the unit interval; cone angles are half angles in radians; orientations are unit quaternions [x, y, z, w]. The simulated world's frame is not the frame of the robots' endpoint's poses: robot.move_arm takes poses in the robot's own frame, whose origin is the point the robot's base stands on, the point scene.get_robots_list reports as position, with +X the way the robot faces. For an upright robot, as every robot that joined the simulation is, a point p in that robot's own frame stands in the simulated world at position + Rz(yaw) * p, where Rz(yaw) turns p about +Z by the robot's listed yaw: [p.x * cos(yaw) - p.y * sin(yaw), p.x * sin(yaw) + p.y * cos(yaw), p.z]. A robot's name here is its name on the robots' endpoint: robot.list there names the same robots, and every tool there takes that name. Keep scale between 0.01 and 100. A setter validates its whole request before it changes anything, a refused request changes nothing in the simulation and says why in message, and every setter answers with the effective value after the call, so read the response rather than assuming the request took. Placement rule: the simulation refuses a spawn (scene.spawn_object), an object move (scene.move_object) and a robot move (scene.move_robot) when a colliding part of what it places would sink more than 5 mm into anything else that collides. That can be the floor, an object, a robot or a fixed piece of the scene. The refusal names each thing it would sink into, with the depth in metres. The placement rule checks only these three calls, at the moment they place something. The placement rule does not measure a soft object or an object with physics none. It never refuses one for what that object sinks into, and you can place other things into it. Support rule: a dynamic or soft object that only rests on a support is not carried when scene.move_object moves that support, scene.remove_object removes it, or controls.set_object_control moves it with a control in m or rad. The object stays where it is and, when the clock runs, falls if nothing holds it up. A static object, an object with physics none and an object with a fixed base never fall. Stance: a standing robot, such as openarm_v1 or openarm_v2, rests on its own base. When the clock runs, it drops straight down until some part of it rests on something. A mounted robot, such as so101, is fixed where it is placed, z included, even in the air, and puts no load on what is under it. A scene load or a scene clear leaves every robot where it stands, with its joint positions, even where an object of the new scene now stands in it. When the clock runs, each robot then follows its stance on what is under it now. A scene load or a scene clear restores the authored lighting and every material, the robots' materials too, only when it changes the selected scene or its scale. Otherwise every lighting and material edit stays. An object's controls are what its asset authors, each a scalar in the unit it reports within its min and max. A control stands at its default when its object is spawned, or at the value the loaded scene placed it at. A lighting or materials change shows in every view the simulation renders, the robots' cameras included, and changes no physics. When you find the world in a state you did not command, call scene.recent_calls: it lists the last state-changing calls of this simulation endpoint with the client that made each.",
  },

  // The record of the state-changing calls this endpoint takes: every call
  // of a tool that is not read-only, tasks included, the last 200 of them.
  call_record: {
    tool: "scene.recent_calls",
    description: "List the last calls of this simulation endpoint that could change the simulated world, newest first, up to 200: every mutating tool and every action, with started_at, the client that made the call (its declared name and version, or null), the tool, its arguments as sent (null when larger than 1 kB, with arguments_bytes), outcome (running, completed, failed, cancelled or refused), success, message and duration_ms. Read outcome and success together: a completed call with success false ran to its end and reports a failure. The record holds the calls of MCP clients on this endpoint only; what a robot did through the robots' endpoint, or another node did, is not in it. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
    keep: 200,
  },

  targets: {
    // LOGICAL targets, not instances: each becomes a contract slot of the
    // server, and the launcher binds all seven to the running simulation
    // engine. One target holds one contract, so the seven contracts the
    // engine serves are seven targets.
    scene: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_manipulation",
        tag: "v1",
        // An author pin: the sha256 of the exact contract document this file
        // was written against (`sha256sum simulation/scene_manipulation.json5`
        // in the contracts hub). A launch that resolves other bytes for
        // `scene_manipulation:v1` refuses instead of publishing schemas this
        // file never saw.
        sha256: "796a0555d610582e749145e1bf9b3a34a21972eb1947603125ea14d956737dff",
      },

      // The catalogue and the inventory become tools that answer within one
      // request. A whole catalogue or a whole warehouse is more than a
      // result may carry, so both listings take filters and refuse a result
      // above their size cap.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_asset_categories",
          tool: "scene.get_asset_categories",
          description: "Count the assets the simulation can place per category, in categories: kind (scene or object), category and count. Call it first, then scene.get_assets_list with a kind and a category to list the entries of one category. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "get_assets_list",
          tool: "scene.get_assets_list",
          description: "List the assets the simulation can place, as a JSON array in assets_json, narrowed by the request: kind (scene or object), category (one scene.get_asset_categories reports) and query (text the entry's asset_id, display_name or description contains, whatever the case); a field left out or sent empty does not filter. Every entry carries at least asset_id, display_name, kind, category and description, and the engine may add fields of its own. A scene entry may carry work_surfaces, the tops robots work at in that scene: each gives its label, its kind (table, shelf or belt), its top in metres above the floor, robot_position and robot_yaw, the joining spot in front of it where a robot stands to work it (scene.move_robot stands a robot there; a robot that joins without a placement of its own stands at the first free joining spot from the origin, which can be another one), and workable_by, the robot models whose arms reach it from there and, for a model that finds items with a camera, whose camera sees what the arms reach. These values hold for the scene loaded at scale 1. An engine that leaves the key out says nothing about the scene's work surfaces. The asset_id values, scene/<id> and object/<id>, are what scene.load_scene and scene.spawn_object take. A result above 64 kB is refused, and the whole catalogue is larger than that: call scene.get_asset_categories first and list one category at a time. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          max_result_bytes: 65536,
        },
        {
          member: "get_objects_list",
          tool: "scene.get_objects_list",
          description: "List the objects standing in the simulation and where they stand, in objects: object_id (what scene.move_object and scene.remove_object take), asset_id, category, source (spawned for an object a caller spawned, scene for one the loaded scene placed), physics (dynamic, static or none), mass, scale, position (world-frame [x, y, z] in metres) and orientation (a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], in the form scene.spawn_object and scene.move_object take, so a listed orientation can be passed back). The poses are as of timestamp; a dynamic object can still be moving. source and category narrow the list; a field left out or sent empty does not filter. source scene with a furniture category gives the work surfaces of the scene: an object's position and scale here, and the size and top height its asset's description in scene.get_assets_list gives. A result above 64 kB is refused, and a large scene holds more objects than that, so narrow it by source and category. This tool checks a setup and completes no task. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          max_result_bytes: 65536,
        },
        {
          member: "get_scene",
          tool: "scene.get_scene",
          description: "Report the scene the simulation has loaded: asset_id, the scene/<id> scene.load_scene took (or the simulation's default scene after scene.clear_scene), and scale, the uniform scale it stands at. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "get_robots_list",
          tool: "scene.get_robots_list",
          description: "List the robots standing in the simulation, one entry each in robots: robot, the name scene.move_robot takes and the name robot.list reports on the robots' endpoint, where every tool takes it; model, the robot catalogue id it stands as; position, its base's world-frame [x, y, z] in metres; yaw, which way the base faces, in radians about the world's +Z; attached, true for a robot that joined and false for one the world stands itself. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],

      // Each action becomes a tool. For a client that declares the MCP
      // tasks extension the call returns a task handle, `tasks/get` reports
      // its state, and `tasks/cancel` forwards to the action's cancel path;
      // for any other client the goal runs inside the call, which answers
      // with the terminal result. load_scene and spawn_object report their
      // progress (bytes fetched, files ready, bytes ready of bytes in all,
      // receive rate, building) as they go; the other actions report their
      // state transitions and the terminal result.
      //
      // The bounds on scale, mass and duration_s live in the prose: an
      // action's goal fields take no `restrict` (the exposure format
      // narrows service requests only), so the engine's own refusal, with
      // its message, is what a goal outside them meets.
      actions: [
        {
          member: "load_scene",
          tool: "scene.load_scene",
          description: "Replace every object of the simulated scene, the spawned ones and the ones the loaded scene placed, with the objects of the scene asset_id names. asset_id must be of kind scene, a scene/<id> from scene.get_assets_list. scale is a uniform scale applied to the scene, 1 = as authored. After the call, every object of the new scene has a new object_id, also after a reload of the same scene, so list the objects again with scene.get_objects_list before you use an id. A load places every object of the scene new, so a reload of the scene puts back its crushed cartons and broken objects whole. success is false, with message saying why, when the scene is refused or fails to load. A scene the simulation has not cached is downloaded first, which can take minutes on a slow link, and the call reports its progress while it runs. A newer scene.load_scene replaces a load the simulation has not started to build (one still downloading its files, or waiting for an earlier load to be built), which then ends as cancelled with a message naming the newer load; a load the simulation already builds is finished first, and the newer one follows it.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // A load fetches the scene's files first, for as long as the link
          // needs, so it is bounded by its progress rather than by its
          // length: it fails only when a whole minute passes with no
          // progress message.
          progress_timeout_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "clear_scene",
          tool: "scene.clear_scene",
          description: "Clear the simulated scene. After the call, no object stands in the simulation, the ones the loaded scene placed included, and the selected scene is scene/default at scale 1. Takes no arguments.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "spawn_object",
          tool: "scene.spawn_object",
          description: "Place one instance of an object asset in the simulation and report the object_id the engine minted for it. asset_id is an object/<id> from scene.get_assets_list; position is world-frame [x, y, z] in metres; yaw turns the object about the world's +Z in radians, 0 = as authored; orientation, when given, is the object's whole orientation in the world frame as a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], in the form scene.get_objects_list reports, and yaw is then ignored (leave it out to stand the object as authored, turned by yaw); scale is uniform, 1 = as authored; physics is dynamic (simulated, falls and collides), static (collides, never moves) or none (visual only); mass is the kilograms of a dynamic object, between 0 and 1000, and is ignored otherwise. After the call, the object stands at position, where it starts: when the clock runs, a dynamic object falls and ends wherever it comes to rest. A heavy object set or dropped on a crushable carton can crush it, and the crush stays; a weight dropped on a brittle object can break it. The load that crushes a crushable carton scales with the mass you spawn it with, so a carton spawned light crushes under a light load, and a carton spawned static never crushes. The spawn is refused under the placement rule of this endpoint's instructions, and a quaternion whose length is off by more than 0.001 is refused. object_id is empty on failure, when the asset is unknown, an argument is invalid or the spawn fails. An object the simulation has not cached is downloaded first, and the call reports its progress while it runs.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // A spawn fetches the object's files first, so, like load_scene,
          // it is bounded by its progress rather than by its length.
          progress_timeout_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_object",
          tool: "scene.move_object",
          description: "Teleport an object in the simulation to a world-frame position [x, y, z] in metres. object_id is an id scene.spawn_object reported or scene.get_objects_list lists. orientation, when given, is the object's new orientation in the world frame as a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], in the form scene.get_objects_list reports, and turns the object about its own origin; left out, the object keeps its orientation. Objects the loaded scene joins to the object, by a weld or by hanging from it, move with it. After the call, a free dynamic or soft object stands still at its new pose, with every object that moves with it. A move of a static object, of one with physics none or of one with a fixed base keeps the velocities of the objects joined to it and of its own moving parts. The placement rule of this endpoint's instructions counts what rests on the object too: a move that pushes the object into it is refused and names it, so move that object first. A move of an object that hangs from another, or of a free object joined to a fixed one, is refused and names the object to move instead. A quaternion whose length is off by more than 0.001 is refused, and so is an orientation on a soft object or on an object joined to another one.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "remove_object",
          tool: "scene.remove_object",
          description: "Delete an object from the simulation. object_id is an id scene.spawn_object reported or scene.get_objects_list lists. After the call, the world is rebuilt without the object and its scene joints, and nothing is checked: every object joined to it, by a weld or by hanging from it, is released. A robot that stood on the removed object then follows its stance of this endpoint's instructions when the clock runs. A work surface the loaded scene declared on the removed object is gone from workspace.describe. You can remove any object, the objects the loaded scene placed included; only an unknown object_id is refused.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "apply_force",
          tool: "scene.apply_force",
          description: "Push a dynamic object in the simulation with a constant world-frame force [x, y, z] in newtons. object_id is any id scene.get_objects_list lists, the objects the loaded scene placed included. After the call, the push acts for duration_s seconds of simulated time, above 0 and at most 30, from the next physics step, so it waits while the simulation is paused. A new push on the same object replaces the old one. Removing the object, loading a scene or clearing the scene cancels the push; moving the object does not. The push is refused on an object whose physics is static or none, and on a dynamic object with a fixed base. The tool reports completion once the push is scheduled, not when it ends.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: this one sets something in motion.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_robot",
          tool: "scene.move_robot",
          description: "Relocate one simulated robot's base, named by robot as scene.get_robots_list reports it, to the world-frame point [x, y, z] in metres, facing yaw in radians about the world's +Z; every other robot stays where it stands. To move a robot without turning it, pass back the yaw scene.get_robots_list reports for it. After the call, the robot keeps its joint positions and the targets its limbs last received, so a robot moved during a motion goes on toward its last target. The robot then follows its stance of this endpoint's instructions from the point: for a standing robot, z only sets where the drop starts and cannot hold the robot above what is under it. An object the robot's gripper holds is not carried: it stays where it is and falls when the clock runs, so put it down before you move the robot. The placement rule of this endpoint's instructions checks the robot at the point itself, before any drop, with its arms in their current pose. So a z that sinks any part of the robot into what is under it past that rule's limit is refused. The move is refused when the point is less than 1.5 m, horizontally, from where another robot is placed, whatever the heights. The robot moves inside the simulation and nowhere else.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: this one moves the (simulated) robot.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
      ],
    },

    controls: {
      contract: {
        name: "object_controls",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/object_controls.json5`).
        sha256: "3cd46653b87f7f0ef0e243c317c12961febce9e449a44adbcc0a80c464ed24c1",
      },

      // The listing is a tool that answers within one request; the set is
      // an action, since a displacement rebuilds the world and a speed
      // waits for the next physics step, and its bounds are per control,
      // so they live in the listing and the engine's refusal rather than
      // in a `restrict`.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_object_controls",
          tool: "controls.get_object_controls",
          description: "List every object of the simulation that offers a control, the objects the loaded scene placed included, in objects: object_id (an id scene.get_objects_list lists), asset_id and controls, each with id (what controls.set_object_control takes), label, unit (m, m/s, rad), min and max (inclusive bounds), default and value (the current one). Bounds, default and value are world quantities: a length control on an object at scale 2 reports twice its authored range. An object whose asset offers no control is not listed; an asset's description in scene.get_assets_list says when it offers one. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
      actions: [
        {
          member: "set_object_control",
          tool: "controls.set_object_control",
          description: "Set one control of an object in the simulation: object_id and control are ids controls.get_object_controls lists, value a number in the control's unit within its min and max. After the call, a control in m or rad stands its part at the new value, like a teleport, even into another object: nothing checks what the part moves into. What rests on that part follows the support rule of this endpoint's instructions, so set such a control before you place things on the part. A control in m/s runs its part's surface at the new speed from the next physics step, a negative value the other way, and carries what rests on it. An unknown object or control, or a value that is not finite or is out of bounds, is refused. Answers with current_value, the value the control stands at after the call, 0 when the object or the control is unknown.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
      ],
    },

    lighting: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_lighting",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/scene_lighting.json5`).
        sha256: "9e66157ee1882c4f06f6cbbfadb02170603366c4562b671be6adc0849b10dd89",
      },

      // Each service becomes a tool that answers within one request. The
      // setters are `mutating`; `restrict` narrows the scalar request
      // fields at the endpoint, before the Peppy graph is touched, and the
      // engine's own per-light bounds apply inside them.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_lighting",
          tool: "lighting.get_lighting",
          description: "Report every light of the loaded simulated scene as a JSON object in lighting_json: scene (the scene asset id), ambient_lux, fill_lux and lights, each with id, kind, label and properties keyed by property name (illuminance_lux, luminous_flux_lm, radiance_scale, color_rgb, position_m, direction, inner_angle_rad, outer_angle_rad, orientation_xyzw), each with unit, default, value and, for scalars, min and max. A light lists only the properties it supports. success is false, with message saying why, while no scene is loaded. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_intensity",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_intensity",
          description: "Set a simulated light's strength: illuminance in lux for a directional light, luminous flux in lumens for a point or spot lamp, or the radiance scale of an environment map (the map's stored radiance times this scale, in cd/m2). light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. After the call, the light's strength is current_value, the value lighting.get_lighting then reports. value must be finite, nonnegative and at most the light's reported max.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          // The contract's `f64` is unbounded; the endpoint accepts a
          // nonnegative value up to ten million, the largest max any light
          // kind reports (10 Mlm for a lamp), and the light's own reported
          // max (200 klux for a directional light, 100 000 for a radiance
          // scale) applies inside that.
          restrict: { value: { min: 0, max: 10000000 } },
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_color",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_color",
          description: "Set a simulated light's colour, color = linear RGB [r, g, b] in the unit interval. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. After the call, the light has the colour current_color. Not supported by an environment light, whose colour is its map.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_position",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_position",
          description: "Set where a simulated point or spot lamp sits, position = world-frame [x, y, z] in metres, Z up. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. After the call, the lamp sits at current_position. A directional or environment light has no position and refuses.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_direction",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_direction",
          description: "Set which way a simulated directional light or spot lamp shines, direction = a world-frame vector [x, y, z] pointing the way the light travels. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. After the call, the light shines along current_direction, a unit vector. A vector with no length is refused, and a point or environment light has no direction and refuses.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_cone",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_cone",
          description: "Set a simulated spot lamp's cone, half angles in radians: full intensity inside inner_angle, none beyond outer_angle, with 0 <= inner_angle < outer_angle < pi/2. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. After the call, the lamp's cone has the half angles current_inner_angle and current_outer_angle.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          // Half angles below pi/2; the ordering of the two is the engine's
          // to check.
          restrict: {
            inner_angle: { min: 0, max: 1.5707 },
            outer_angle: { min: 0, max: 1.5707 },
          },
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_orientation",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_orientation",
          description: "Set a simulated environment map's orientation, orientation = a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w] rotating the map into the world frame. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. After the call, the environment map is turned by current_orientation. Only an environment light supports it.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "reset_lighting",
          tool: "lighting.reset_lighting",
          description: "Reset every light of the loaded simulated scene. After the call, every light of the loaded scene has its authored values. Takes no arguments.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
    },

    materials: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_materials",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/scene_materials.json5`).
        sha256: "ff2ce6127fdc52c97bf3e743297a31ec8bea2d153085baf8ad34e8d1112e2953",
      },

      // Services again, the setters `mutating`; `restrict` narrows the
      // finish's two scalars to the unit interval at the endpoint.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_materials",
          tool: "materials.get_materials",
          description: "Report every editable material of the loaded simulated scene as a JSON object in materials_json: materials, each with id, label, scope (owner, a robot instance or the scene; surfaces, the count of surfaces sharing it; bodies, their body names) and properties (color_rgb, metallic, roughness), each with unit, default and value, plus min and max for the scalars. Every surface sharing a material changes with it, so the scope is what an edit reaches. success is false, with message saying why, while no scene is loaded. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_material_color",
          tool: "materials.set_material_color",
          description: "Set a simulated material's base colour, color = linear RGB reflectance [r, g, b] in the unit interval. material_id comes from materials.get_materials. After the call, every surface that shares the material has the base colour current_color.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_material_finish",
          tool: "materials.set_material_finish",
          description: "Set a simulated material's finish: metallic and roughness, both in the unit interval (0 metallic a dielectric, 1 a conductor; 0 roughness a mirror finish, 1 fully diffuse). material_id comes from materials.get_materials. After the call, every surface that shares the material has the finish current_metallic and current_roughness.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          // The contract's `f64` is unbounded; the metallic-roughness model
          // is defined on the unit interval.
          restrict: {
            metallic: { min: 0, max: 1 },
            roughness: { min: 0, max: 1 },
          },
        },
        {
          member: "reset_materials",
          tool: "materials.reset_materials",
          description: "Reset every material of the loaded simulated scene. After the call, every material of the loaded scene has its authored values on every surface that shares it. Takes no arguments.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
    },

    view: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_view",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/scene_view.json5`).
        sha256: "99a9778c807f1c4492bc7299223d10192f6440f9d5aff93c421b6199c75f6954",
      },

      // Each service answers with a picture: its frame is the image block
      // of the result, the rest of the response the document, as a camera's
      // picture tool answers, under the same representation and size
      // policies. A refusal carries no frame and reaches the client as the
      // document alone.
      services: [
        {
          member: "render_view",
          tool: "scene.look",
          description: "Look at the simulated world from a free viewpoint: a JPEG picture of the world as it stands now, seen from position, a world-frame point [x, y, z] in metres, looking at target, another, with the world's +Z up, and timestamp, the simulated instant it shows. The picture is for checking a setup: no robot sees it, and the pictures the robots' own cameras see are the robots' endpoint's camera.look and depth_camera.look. A position equal to the target, or a line of sight straight along the vertical, is refused; success false with a message is also the answer of a simulation that runs without rendering. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 10000,
          representation: {
            image: "jpeg",
            quality: 80,
            fields: {
              data: "frame",
              encoding: "encoding",
              width: "width",
              height: "height",
            },
          },
          // The frame is a variable-length byte array, so the runtime size
          // policy is mandatory.
          max_result_bytes: 524288,
          on_oversize: "downscale",
        },
        {
          member: "preview_asset",
          tool: "scene.preview_asset",
          description: "See an asset of the catalogue before you place it: a JPEG picture of the asset asset_id (an id scene.get_assets_list lists, scene/<id> or object/<id>) as the catalogue shows it, an object standing alone as authored, a scene seen whole, over a white background. It shows the asset, not the simulated world: the world as it stands is scene.look. The first call for an asset fetches its preview from the asset store. An unknown asset_id, or an asset the catalogue holds no preview for, is refused with the reason. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 20000,
          representation: {
            image: "jpeg",
            quality: 80,
            fields: {
              data: "frame",
              encoding: "encoding",
              width: "width",
              height: "height",
            },
          },
          max_result_bytes: 524288,
          on_oversize: "downscale",
        },
      ],
    },

    clock: {
      contract: {
        name: "simulation_clock",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/simulation_clock.json5`).
        sha256: "a791c83a079bbd1b0fe9ac638e926b23a3248fab696027f5934e1083502209b1",
      },

      // Two services and one action: the step waits for its physics steps
      // to run, up to 30 simulated seconds at the normal pace.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_clock",
          tool: "clock.get_clock",
          description: "Report the simulation's clock: now, the simulated time, on the same clock as the timestamp of every message of this simulation (a stamp compared with it says how old the stamp is), and paused, whether the simulation stands still. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_paused",
          tool: "clock.set_paused",
          description: "Pause the simulation (paused true) or resume it (paused false). After the call, while paused is true the simulation runs no physics step and its clock stands still, until clock.step or a resume with paused false. While paused, every scene, controls, lighting and materials tool still answers, and an edit shows at once in scene.get_objects_list, in scene.look and in the robots' cameras. A robot cannot move while the simulation is paused: its backbone runs on wall time and sees its limbs go silent, so a robot move in flight fails and one sent while paused fails too; resume before a robot moves. The deadlines of every tool are wall time and keep running. Setting the state the simulation is already in succeeds and changes nothing. Answers with now and paused, the state after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
      actions: [
        {
          member: "step",
          tool: "clock.step",
          description: "Advance a paused simulation by duration_s seconds of simulated time, rounded up to whole physics steps, at the simulation's normal pace (so a step of 10 s takes about 10 s); use it to let objects settle after placing them. During a step the whole physics runs: the robots' servos drive toward their last targets, and pushes and belts act too. After the call, the simulation is paused again, and nothing checks that things have come to rest, so check with scene.get_objects_list. Refused while the simulation runs (pause it first with clock.set_paused), while a step is running, and for a duration that is not above 0 or is above 30. Answers with now, the simulated time the step reached.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
      ],
    },

    // Where a robot can work, measured in the world: the surfaces its arms
    // reach and its perception camera sees, with what stands in the way,
    // and whether given objects are workable where they stand. Like
    // scene.get_objects_list it checks a setup and completes no task, and
    // like scene.get_objects_list it answers only while Waldo runs its debug
    // inspector.
    workspace: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_workspace",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/scene_workspace.json5`).
        sha256: "91465d7ccb24177b6eeb9191ea88ac45f6fdf77c8029a81372bf29b6b0ad26ab",
      },

      // Two services. A call solves the arms' reach for each target Waldo
      // does not yet keep for the robot's model: each grid point on a top it
      // measures (each surface workspace.describe measures, and the top under
      // an object workspace.check cannot work) and each checked object's
      // centre. That takes about 14 s for an OpenArm v2 at the living room's
      // coffee table, hence the deadline. Each answer lists surfaces or
      // objects, so it takes the listings' size cap.
      services: [
        {
          member: "describe_workspace",
          tool: "workspace.describe",
          description: "Learn where a robot can work in the simulated world, measured in it: for each work surface the loaded scene declares whose object still stands (the work_surfaces scene.get_assets_list gives for the scene) and for each object of surface_object_ids besides (a table you spawned, its top the top of its bounding box), whether the robot named by robot, a name scene.get_robots_list gives, reaches and sees enough of it to work there, and where on it to put objects. It is for setting up and checking a simulated world: a robot's task still finds its items through the robot's own perception. A point of a top is workable when an arm brings its grasp point within 0.01 m of a target 0.04 m above it with the gripper pointing straight down or straight forward, solved from the pose the robot's model starts in, and the robot's perception camera, its one depth camera no arm carries, sees it with nothing that stays where it is in the way; a robot without a perception camera is judged on reach alone. Only points from 0 to 1.0 m ahead of the robot and up to 0.30 m to either side are measured. Answers with robot, perception_camera, robot_position and robot_yaw (where the robot's base stands, world-frame [x, y, z] in metres, and the yaw it faces, in radians about the world's +Z), and surfaces, the declared ones in the scene's order, then the requested ones: label (the declared label, or the object's asset id), object_id, declared, top_found and top (the height in metres the rays found the top at, 0 when they found none; they look for a declared surface's top within 0.1 m of its declared height, so name an object raised or lowered further, by its controls for example, in surface_object_ids), workable (true when its workable points cover at least 0.03 square metres), area (the workable part in square metres), center and corners (the world-frame centre [x, y, z] in metres of the largest rectangle of workable points on the top, and its four corners, 3 values each, counter-clockwise seen from above from the one nearest the robot on its right: put objects inside them; both empty when no point is workable) and message, one line that says why. A result above 64 kB is refused: name a few dozen objects at most in surface_object_ids per call. It answers only while Waldo runs its debug inspector, and refuses otherwise, saying so; a robot that does not stand in the world, a robot model whose arms or perception camera cannot be measured, an unknown object id and an object with no bounding box (neither colliding geometry nor a soft surface) are refused too. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
          max_result_bytes: 65536,
        },
        {
          member: "check_objects",
          tool: "workspace.check",
          description: "Learn whether a robot can work each of the given objects where it stands in the simulated world: robot is a name scene.get_robots_list gives, and object_ids holds at least one id scene.get_objects_list lists. It is for setting up and checking a simulated world: a robot's task still finds its items through the robot's own perception. An arm reaches for the centre of an object's bounding box, and workable, reachable and in_view are as workspace.describe defines them, except that anything but the robot's own body hides an object from the perception camera. Answers with perception_camera, all_workable, whether the robot can work every object, and objects, one per id in the request's order: object_id; asset_id; position, the world-frame centre [x, y, z] of its bounding box in metres; resting_on, the object it rests on: followed down through the free objects it rests on, the first that stands fixed or that the scene declares as a work surface (a book on a book on a table rests on the table), or, when that chain reaches a floor first (the world's floor, or an object that stands fixed where it is touched at the floor's height, such as a room's floor slab or a doorway's threshold), the lowest object of the chain above the floor (a cup on a side table that can move rests on the side table), empty when it rests on a floor itself or on nothing, and for an object that stands fixed, part of the furniture; workable, reachable and in_view; arm, the arm that reaches it, by the name the robots' endpoint's limb tools take, empty when none does; short_by, how far in metres the closest arm's grasp point stops short of it whichever way the gripper points, 0 when an arm reaches it, and at most 0.01 with reachable false when the arms come that close but none reaches it with its gripper pointing down or forward; view, seen, outside_field, out_of_depth, hidden, or no_camera for a robot without a perception camera; hidden_by, what stands in the way when view is hidden, empty otherwise; suggested_position, the workable world-frame point [x, y, z] in metres on the top of the object it rests on that lies nearest to it, where to move an object the robot cannot work, empty when the object is workable, rests on no object, or that top has no workable point, and empty too when the point under the object is that nearest point, since what keeps it from being workable then (what hides it, or the height of its centre) stays where it is; and message, one line that says why and where to move it. A result above 64 kB is refused, and about a hundred objects are larger than that: check a large set of objects a few dozen at a time. It answers only while Waldo runs its debug inspector, and refuses otherwise, saying so; an empty object_ids, a robot that does not stand in the world, a robot model whose arms or perception camera cannot be measured, an unknown object id and an object with no bounding box (neither colliding geometry nor a soft surface) are refused too. Changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
          max_result_bytes: 65536,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
